## Context

Author idea (2026-06-10): make the "long tail" claim empirical and define
"complete" operationally per use case. Strengthens the new §2 empirical
Related Work (why-we-redo-GEM, ticket 0512) and prefigures the data paper
(0517). Uses ONLY existing committed data — no new API runs.

## What to do

1. **Long-tail figure**: X = 177 reference plants, sorted by visibility;
   Y = recognition layers — known to Gold (reference, by construction),
   GEM, Wikipedia, OSM (layer exists in fuse_runs.py — verify coverage),
   and the model census (recognition matrix). One glance shows the
   documented head and the under-documented tail. Script in
   `src/aedist/plot_*.py`, PDF artifact wired in Makefile
   (figures-are-artifacts rule). Degrade gracefully if an OSM layer is
   too sparse — drop the row, do not fake it.

2. **Statistical perimeters paragraph** (in §2 empirical RW): which
   lifecycle perimeter serves which use:
   - Market dispatch → operational only
   - Investment analysis → + under construction + planned (with critical
     reading)
   - Development planning / modelling → + proposed
   - Policy analysis, energy-transition studies → + cancelled/abandoned
   This gives "complete" an operational definition per audience and
   motivates keeping the speculative tail (consistent with 0498's
   superset framing).

3. Reference the figure from the why-we-redo-GEM paragraph (0512).

## Exit criteria

- [ ] plot script + Makefile target produce the long-tail PDF from
      committed artifacts
- [ ] Layer counts in the caption re-derived from the generated CSV
      (derive-prose-from-artifacts rule), guarded by an adherence test
- [ ] Perimeters paragraph in §2 empirical RW
- [ ] Figure referenced from why-we-redo-GEM paragraph
- [ ] `make check` passes

